## Founding & History
Pooley Wines was established in 1985 by Jack 'Denis' and Margaret Pooley, who planted seven rows of Pinot Noir and ten rows of Riesling on their 16-hectare farm at Cooinda Vale, located on the banks of the Coal River in southern Tasmania. This initial planting was intended as a retirement project, but the vineyard quickly developed into a renowned cool-climate site. The Cooinda Vale vineyard yielded its first vintage in 1989.

Jack Pooley continued working in the vineyard until his passing in 1994. In 2003, their son John Pooley and his wife Libby purchased Belmont, a heritage-listed Georgian home on the outskirts of Richmond. This acquisition led to the expansion of Pooley Wines with the establishment of a second vineyard, Butcher's Hill, which produced its first vintage in 2007. Margaret Pooley remained the chief executive of the Cooinda Vale Vineyard until she passed away in 2010. By 2013, with Margaret's granddaughter Anna Pooley returning to the family business, Pooley Wines became entirely managed by family members.

## People
Pooley Wines is a three-generation family business. The founders were Jack 'Denis' and Margaret Pooley. Their son, John Pooley, and his wife Libby, expanded the estate by purchasing Belmont in 2003.

Matthew Pooley, grandson of Denis and Margaret and son of John, serves as the Viticulturist, having worked with the family since the 1990s. He studied a Diploma in Farm Business Management and returned to Tasmania in 1994 to manage the family property.

Anna Pooley, granddaughter of Denis and Margaret and daughter of John, is the principal winemaker. She returned to the family business in 2013, bringing experience from other Australian wineries and international work in Tuscany and Austria. Anna's husband, Justin Bubb, also works as a winemaker for Pooley Wines. Anna and Matt Pooley were named finalists for the 2018 GT Winemaker of The Year. Anna was also named one of the Wine Business Magazine Top 50 Most Influential Wine People of 2009 and Australian Young Winemaker of the Year by the Wine Society in 2010. She completed the Len Evans Tutorial in 2012.

## Vineyard & Site
Pooley Wines operates two vineyards in the Coal River Valley: Cooinda Vale and Butcher's Hill.

The Cooinda Vale vineyard, established in 1985, is 16 hectares and is positioned facing northeast, overlooking the Coal River. It is located at approximately 43.5 degrees Latitude and planted on northeast-facing slopes at an elevation of 80m-130m above sea level. The site is described as a unique microclimatic location, protected from prevailing winds but influenced by maritime conditions. The soil type at Cooinda Vale consists of sandy loams and sandy clay loam over impervious clay. Initial plantings included Pinot Noir and Riesling.

The Butcher's Hill vineyard was established in 2003 on the Belmont property near Richmond. The slope on which Belmont sits is heritage-listed, as it was the source of sandstone for Richmond village and its famous bridge.

Pooley Wines is recognized for its environmental approach to winemaking, being the first and only fully credited environmentally sustainable vineyards in Tasmania. Their philosophy includes a commitment to sustainable viticulture.

## Cellar Door Experience
The Pooley Wines cellar door is located on the outskirts of the historic Richmond township, in the heart of the Coal River Valley. It is housed in The Coach House, which was originally the coach house and barn of the historic Belmont estate, a heritage-listed, convict-built Georgian home dating back to circa 1832. The cellar door itself is also heritage-listed.

Visitors can enjoy in-depth structured tastings. The cellar door offers local cheese and charcuterie boards daily, and artisan wood-fired pizzas on weekends. The facility is family-friendly and offers alfresco/outdoor dining. Disabled access is available.

## Food & Hospitality
The cellar door offers local cheese and charcuterie boards daily, and artisan wood-fired pizzas on weekends. Pooley Wines also provides luxury accommodation and fine dining across the road at the restored Prospect Country House and Buscombe's Restaurant, which features 11 luxuriously appointed rooms. Buscombe's Restaurant offers a setting for connection, celebration, and indulgence.

## Awards & Recognition
Pooley Wines was awarded the 2023 Winery of the Year by the Halliday Wine Companion. James Halliday noted the "breathtaking" ascension of their wines' quality since Anna and Justin Bubb returned in 2012. The winery has received numerous trophies and medals at prestigious wine shows.

Anna Pooley, the winemaker, has received individual accolades, including being named one of the Wine Business Magazine Top 50 Most Influential Wine People of 2009 and Australian Young Winemaker of the Year by the Wine Society in 2010. Anna and Matt Pooley were finalists for the 2018 GT Winemaker of The Year.

Specific wine ratings include 95 points for the 2022 Jack Denis Pooley Pinot Noir from Aaron Brasher of The Real Review. The Pooley Riesling 2024 received 93/100, the Pooley Margaret Pooley Tribute Riesling 2023 received 97/100, and the Pooley Cooinda Vale Pinot Noir 2023 received 92/100. The 2024 Cooinda Vale Pinot Noir received 94 points.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Pooley Wines is recognized as the first and only fully credited environmentally sustainable vineyard in Tasmania. They hold Sustainable Tourism Accreditation by ATIC and Sustainable Winegrowing Australia Certification. Their philosophy emphasizes a commitment to sustainable viticulture. They are also members of Sustainable Winegrowing Australia and Wine Tasmania.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Pooley Wines is 1431 Richmond Road, Richmond, Tasmania, 7025.
The phone number is (03) 6260 2895.
The website is http://www.pooleywines.com.au/.
The cellar door is located on the outskirts of the historic Richmond township. It is approximately 35km north of Hobart.
The cellar door is pet-friendly (enquire for details) and offers online orders and takeaway options.

## Notable / Distinctive
Pooley Wines holds the distinction of being the first three-generation Tasmanian wine family. They are also noted as the first and only fully credited environmentally sustainable vineyards in Tasmania. The cellar door is housed in the historic Coach House of Belmont, a heritage-listed Georgian home, with the property's sandstone having been quarried to build Richmond village and its famous bridge. The winery was awarded the prestigious 2023 Winery of the Year by the Halliday Wine Companion.

